// The carrier of the mutual-TLS example: the standard (:tcp) carrier, compiler/stdlib/dangerous_tcp_carrier.silica,
// with the socket wrappers replaced by the TLS wrappers of dangerous_tls. Everything below the module line is that
// carrier's text (as of 2026-11-12, plus the connect timeout of 2026-11-16, below) with dangerous_sockets@ read
// as dangerous_tls@: the carrier contract of transport_serialization_design.md 6.2 is the same, message for
// message, so the program that owns this carrier is written exactly as it would be over plain TCP. That is the
// point of the example: mutual TLS sits BELOW the carrier contract, in the wrapper, and a program gains it by
// naming another carrier.
//
// It is opened with (:open, owner, address):
//   address "host:port:timeout_ms"  dials the peer and waits at most timeout_ms milliseconds for the TCP connect
//                                   and the TLS handshake together: a connection carrier (the connect timeout, below);
//   address "listen:port"           binds 127.0.0.1:port and becomes a listener;
//   address "listen:any:port"       binds 0.0.0.0:port, every IPv4 interface of the host, and becomes a listener.
//
// The connect timeout (hosted Ethernet plan stage E8, as the standard carrier's). A dial states how long the
// carrier may wait for the peer: the third part of "host:port:timeout_ms", whole milliseconds written as 1 to 9
// decimal digits, greater than 0. There is no default. An address without the third part ("host:port"), with an
// empty one, with anything but digits in it, with more than 9 digits or with the value 0 is malformed: nothing is
// dialled, the owner is told (:link_down, :fault) and the carrier ends with (:explicit, :bad_address). The dial
// never blocks: the socket is non-blocking while it connects and while the client's handshake runs, and the
// carrier's worker waits in polls that end at one deadline, timeout_ms after the dial began
// (dangerous_tls@tcp_connect). A peer that does not answer the connect in that time (a firewall that drops the
// packets, a host that is down), or that accepts the connection and does not finish the handshake in it, is a
// timed-out open: the owner is told (:link_down, :timeout) and the carrier ends with (:explicit, :link_timeout).
// A refused connection (nothing listens on the port) and a failed handshake are a failed open:
// (:link_down, :fault), and the carrier ends with (:explicit, :link_unavailable). The carrier handles no other
// message while it waits, so the timeout is also the longest time a :close sent during the dial stays unread.
// The :link_up of a dialled connection carries the peer, "host:port", without the timeout.
//
// The carrier ends with its link, exactly as the standard carrier does (transport_serialization_design.md 6.2,
// transport_links_design.md 6.3): :link_down is the last message its owner receives, the descriptor and its TLS
// state are released (a TLS close_notify is sent to the peer on :close and on a failed write), and a supervisor
// restarts a faulted link and leaves a closed one alone. Event -> owner message -> exit reason:
//   :close from the owner                        (:link_down, :closed)    :normal
//   peer closed (close_notify or dropped link)   (:link_down, :closed)    :normal
//   read failed (fatal alert, reset)             (:link_down, :fault)     (:explicit, :link_lost)
//   write failed or timed out                    (:tx_failed, :link_down), (:link_down, :fault)  (:explicit, :link_lost)
//   :tx on a carrier that is not up              (:tx_failed, :link_down) only: the carrier goes on
//   dial refused (nothing listens)               (:link_down, :fault)     (:explicit, :link_unavailable)
//   dial handshake failed (certificate refused)  (:link_down, :fault)     (:explicit, :link_unavailable)
//   dial connect or handshake timed out          (:link_down, :timeout)   (:explicit, :link_timeout)
//   dial address malformed (no stated timeout)   (:link_down, :fault)     (:explicit, :bad_address)
//   listener cannot bind                         (:link_down, :fault)     (:explicit, :link_unavailable)
// (In TLS 1.3 a server that refuses the client's certificate says so only after the client's handshake has
// finished: the client then ends as a read that failed, (:link_down, :fault) and :link_lost, not as a failed dial.)
//
// What the owner sees that it would not see over plain TCP:
//   - a dial to a peer that does not accept this node, or that this node does not accept, ends with
//     (:link_down, :fault), as a refused connection does (the wrapper says why on standard error);
//   - a listener never announces a peer that failed the handshake: there is no (:accepted, ...) for it;
//   - in TLS 1.3 a server that refuses the client's certificate says so after the client's own handshake has
//     finished, so the refused client sees (:link_up, ...) and then (:link_down, :fault) on its first read.
//
// This is an example's carrier, started with spawn_dangerous and not declared with carried_on: the language's
// carrier kinds are the compiler's, and TLS is not one of them. Silica's secure interchange is being built in
// Project BEES (https://github.com/Silica-Language-and-Compiler/project-bees), TRUST over mutual TLS 1.3 with a
// certificate whitelist, which is the trust rule this example's wrapper applies.
//
// A connection carrier answers :link_up, grants 8 send credits, and then moves bytes: each :tx consumes one
// credit and :tx_credit hands it back once the bytes are written; received bytes are delivered as :rx only
// while the owner has granted :credit. A listener answers (:link_up, "port:<n>") once bound and, for every
// peer that passes the handshake, spawns one connection carrier and tells its owner
// (:accepted, conn: dangerous_actor_ref, "peer:accepted"); whoever sends that carrier (:open, owner, _)
// becomes the connection's owner. A carrier polls its descriptor between mailbox messages with a 20 ms
// timeout. A dial (connect and handshake) waits for at most the timeout its address states; a listener's
// handshake with an accepted peer is bounded at 5 seconds for each read and write and runs on the listener's
// thread.
module dangerous_tls_carrier;

use dangerous_tls;

export initial/0;
export carrier/2;

fn initial() -> { owner: actor_ref, fd: int64, rx_credit: int64, up: boolean, listening: boolean } {
    { owner: actor_ref_of_word(0), fd: 0 - 1, rx_credit: 0, up: false, listening: false }
}

fn starts(s: string, p: string) -> boolean {
    length_bytes(s) >= length_bytes(p) and substring(s, 0, length_bytes(p)) == p
}

// byte_at is a transport operation (specification §9.1.1, §16.4): called inside a sequence that
// declares `transport`, here, so the address parsers stay pure.
fn at(s: string, i: int64) -> int64 {
    sequence proc[transport]
        b: int64 <- byte_at(s, i)
    produces
        pure b
    end
}

fn digits_from(s: string, i: int64, acc: int64) -> int64 {
    case i >= length_bytes(s) of {
        true -> acc;
        false -> {
            d: int64 <- at(s, i) - 48;
            case d < 0 or d > 9 of {
                true -> acc;
                false -> digits_from(s, i + 1, acc * 10 + d)
            }
        }
    }
}

fn host_of(address: string) -> string {
    substring_until_char(address, 0, ':')
}

fn port_of(address: string) -> int64 {
    h: string <- host_of(address);
    digits_from(address, length_bytes(h) + 1, 0)
}

// Where the timeout text of a dial address "host:port:timeout_ms" starts, or -1 when the address has no third
// part (no second ':' or nothing after it).
fn connect_timeout_start(address: string) -> int64 {
    port_start: int64 <- length_bytes(host_of(address)) + 1;
    case port_start >= length_bytes(address) of {
        true -> 0 - 1;
        false -> {
            port_text: string <- substring_until_char(address, port_start, ':');
            start: int64 <- port_start + length_bytes(port_text) + 1;
            case start >= length_bytes(address) of {
                true -> 0 - 1;
                false -> start
            }
        }
    }
}

fn all_digits_from(s: string, i: int64) -> boolean {
    case i >= length_bytes(s) of {
        true -> true;
        false -> {
            d: int64 <- at(s, i) - 48;
            case d < 0 or d > 9 of {
                true -> false;
                false -> all_digits_from(s, i + 1)
            }
        }
    }
}

// The connect timeout a dial address states, in milliseconds, or 0 when it states none: the third part must be
// 1 to 9 decimal digits and nothing else (header). 0 is never a timeout, so "host:port:0" is refused with the
// malformed ones.
fn connect_timeout_of(address: string) -> int64 {
    start: int64 <- connect_timeout_start(address);
    case start < 0 of {
        true -> 0;
        false -> case length_bytes(address) - start > 9 or all_digits_from(address, start) == false of {
            true -> 0;
            false -> digits_from(address, start, 0)
        }
    }
}

// The peer of a dial address whose timeout is stated: "host:port", what a dialled connection's :link_up carries.
fn dial_peer_text(address: string) -> string {
    substring(address, 0, connect_timeout_start(address) - 1)
}

// The port of a listener address: "listen:any:<port>" or "listen:<port>".
fn listen_port_of(address: string) -> int64 {
    case starts(address, "listen:any:") of {
        true -> digits_from(address, 11, 0);
        false -> port_of(address)
    }
}

fn pure_int(r: (:ok, int64) | (:rejected, atom)) -> int64 {
    case r of {
        (:ok, v: int64) -> v;
        (:rejected, _why: atom) -> 0 - 1
    }
}

fn int_text_from(n: int64, acc: string) -> string {
    case n < 10 of {
        true -> concat(substring("0123456789", n, n + 1), acc);
        false -> int_text_from(n / 10, concat(substring("0123456789", n % 10, n % 10 + 1), acc))
    }
}

fn int_text(n: int64) -> string {
    int_text_from(n, "")
}

// The message read without the worker's effects: pure selectors a sequence can bind.
fn msg_kind(msg: (:open, actor_ref, string) | (:tx, string, actor_ref) | (:credit, int64) | :close | :poll) -> atom {
    case msg of {
        (:open, _o: actor_ref, _a: string) -> :open;
        (:tx, _s: string, _f: actor_ref) -> :tx;
        (:credit, _n: int64) -> :credit;
        :close -> :close;
        :poll -> :poll
    }
}

fn msg_owner(msg: (:open, actor_ref, string) | (:tx, string, actor_ref) | (:credit, int64) | :close | :poll, fallback: actor_ref) -> actor_ref {
    case msg of {
        (:open, o: actor_ref, _a: string) -> o;
        (:tx, _s: string, _f: actor_ref) -> fallback;
        (:credit, _n: int64) -> fallback;
        :close -> fallback;
        :poll -> fallback
    }
}

fn msg_text(msg: (:open, actor_ref, string) | (:tx, string, actor_ref) | (:credit, int64) | :close | :poll) -> string {
    case msg of {
        (:open, _o: actor_ref, a: string) -> a;
        (:tx, s: string, _f: actor_ref) -> s;
        (:credit, _n: int64) -> "";
        :close -> "";
        :poll -> ""
    }
}

fn msg_count(msg: (:open, actor_ref, string) | (:tx, string, actor_ref) | (:credit, int64) | :close | :poll) -> int64 {
    case msg of {
        (:open, _o: actor_ref, _a: string) -> 0;
        (:tx, _s: string, _f: actor_ref) -> 0;
        (:credit, n: int64) -> n;
        :close -> 0;
        :poll -> 0
    }
}

fn none_res() -> { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } {
    { tag: 2, value: 0 - 1, error_code: 0 }
}

// One mailbox message, one pass over the socket: the sequence proc[external_danger] is the whole body
// (the language allows it only there), so the work is chosen by the message and the state and each
// wrapper is called at most once per message. Received bytes reach the owner in the one tainted cast
// a worker may make per message.
//
// The state tells the three roles apart: `listening` is a listener; `up` is a connection carrying
// bytes; a descriptor with neither (fd >= 0, not up, not listening) is a connection a listener
// accepted that is waiting for its (:open, owner, _).
fn carrier(msg: (:open, actor_ref, string) | (:tx, string, actor_ref) | (:credit, int64) | :close | :poll, state: { owner: actor_ref, fd: int64, rx_credit: int64, up: boolean, listening: boolean }) -> (:no_reply, { owner: actor_ref, fd: int64, rx_credit: int64, up: boolean, listening: boolean }) {
    sequence proc[external_danger, concurrency, transport]
        kind: atom <- msg_kind(msg);
        owner: actor_ref <- msg_owner(msg, state.owner);
        text: string <- msg_text(msg);
        adoptable: boolean <- state.fd >= 0 and state.up == false and state.listening == false;
        do_adopt: boolean <- kind == :open and adoptable;
        listen_mode: boolean <- starts(text, "listen:");
        listen_any: boolean <- starts(text, "listen:any:");
        do_connect: boolean <- kind == :open and adoptable == false and listen_mode == false;
        do_listen: boolean <- kind == :open and adoptable == false and listen_mode;
        // The connect timeout a dial states (header): none, or a malformed one, is a malformed address, and
        // then nothing is dialled (none_res: open_tag is not 0, so the open fails below).
        timeout_ms: int64 <- case do_connect of { true -> connect_timeout_of(text); false -> 0 };
        bad_address: boolean <- do_connect and timeout_ms <= 0;
        res_open: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case do_connect and bad_address == false of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@tcp_connect(host_of(text), port_of(text), timeout_ms);
            false -> case do_listen and listen_any of {
                true -> dangerous_tls@tcp_listen_any(listen_port_of(text));
                false -> case do_listen of {
                    true -> dangerous_tls@tcp_listen(listen_port_of(text));
                    false -> none_res()
                }
            }
        };
        open_tag_raw: int64 <- res_open.tag;
        open_fd_raw: int64 <- res_open.value;
        open_tag: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(open_tag_raw));
        open_fd: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(open_fd_raw));
        do_accept: boolean <- kind == :poll and state.listening;
        res_acc: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case do_accept of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@tcp_accept(state.fd, 20);
            false -> none_res()
        };
        acc_tag_raw: int64 <- res_acc.tag;
        acc_fd_raw: int64 <- res_acc.value;
        acc_tag: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(acc_tag_raw));
        acc_fd: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(acc_fd_raw));
        accepted: boolean <- do_accept and acc_tag == 0;
        bound: boolean <- do_listen and open_tag == 0;
        res_port: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case bound of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@local_port(open_fd);
            false -> none_res()
        };
        port_raw: int64 <- res_port.value;
        port: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(port_raw));
        do_tx: boolean <- kind == :tx and state.up;
        res_tx: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case do_tx of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@send_bytes(state.fd, text, 1000);
            false -> none_res()
        };
        tx_tag_raw: int64 <- res_tx.tag;
        tx_tag: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(tx_tag_raw));
        do_rx: boolean <- kind == :poll and state.up and state.listening == false and state.rx_credit > 0;
        res_rx: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case do_rx of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@recv_bytes(state.fd, 65536, 20);
            false -> none_res()
        };
        rx_tag_raw: int64 <- res_rx.tag;
        rx_tag: int64 <- pure_int(recreate(rx_tag_raw));
        got: boolean <- do_rx and rx_tag == 0;
        bytes_in: string <- case got of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@take(state.fd);
            false -> ""
        };
        // The descriptor (and its TLS state: a TLS close_notify to the peer) is given back whenever the link
        // ends, not only on :close: the peer closed or the read failed (a fatal alert, a reset), or a write
        // failed (a peer that went away, or a write that timed out part way and left a torn record).
        open_failed: boolean <- kind == :open and adoptable == false and open_tag != 0;
        rx_ended: boolean <- do_rx and (rx_tag == 1 or rx_tag == 3);
        tx_broke: boolean <- do_tx and tx_tag != 0;
        do_close: boolean <- (kind == :close or rx_ended or tx_broke) and state.fd >= 0;
        _: { tag: int64, value: int64, error_code: int64 } <- case do_close of {
            true -> dangerous_tls@close_fd(state.fd);
            false -> none_res()
        };
        // An accepted peer gets a connection carrier of its own, holding the accepted descriptor;
        // the owner learns of it and chooses who opens it.
        _: boolean <- case accepted of {
            true -> {
                sequence proc[concurrency]
                    conn: dangerous_actor_ref <- spawn_dangerous({ owner: actor_ref_of_word(0), fd: acc_fd, rx_credit: 0, up: false, listening: false }, carrier, stack_policy(0, :keep_last_message), state_policy(:keep_state_area));
                    told: boolean <- cast(owner, (:accepted, conn, "peer:accepted") impl ActorMessage {})
                produces
                    pure told
                end
            };
            false -> true
        };
        // :link_up once when connected, once when a listener is bound ("port:<n>", the port the
        // system chose when asked for 0), and once when an accepted connection is opened.
        connected: boolean <- do_connect and open_tag == 0;
        link_up_now: boolean <- connected or bound or do_adopt;
        link_down_now: boolean <- open_failed or rx_ended or tx_broke or (kind == :close);
        ended_normally: boolean <- kind == :close or (do_rx and rx_tag == 3);
        // A dial that was made and whose peer did not answer the connect, or did not finish the handshake,
        // within the stated timeout (tag 2 of the connect; a malformed address makes no call and is not one).
        connect_timed_out: boolean <- do_connect and bad_address == false and open_tag == 2;
        down_reason: atom <- case kind == :close of {
            true -> :closed;
            false -> case do_rx and rx_tag == 3 of {
                true -> :closed;
                false -> case connect_timed_out of {
                    true -> :timeout;
                    false -> :fault
                }
            }
        };
        _: boolean <- case link_up_now of {
            true -> cast(owner, (:link_up, case bound of { true -> concat("port:", int_text(port)); false -> case do_adopt of { true -> "peer:accepted"; false -> dial_peer_text(text) } }) impl ActorMessage {});
            false -> true
        };
        _: boolean <- case connected or do_adopt of {
            true -> cast(owner, (:tx_credit, 8) impl ActorMessage {});
            false -> true
        };
        _: boolean <- case do_tx of {
            true -> case tx_tag == 0 of {
                true -> cast(owner, (:tx_credit, 1) impl ActorMessage {});
                false -> cast(owner, (:tx_failed, :link_down) impl ActorMessage {})
            };
            false -> case kind == :tx of {
                true -> cast(owner, (:tx_failed, :link_down) impl ActorMessage {});
                false -> true
            }
        };
        _: boolean <- case got of {
            true -> cast(owner, (:rx, bytes_in) impl ActorMessage {});
            false -> true
        };
        _: boolean <- case link_down_now of {
            true -> cast(owner, (:link_down, down_reason) impl ActorMessage {});
            false -> true
        };
        // The carrier ends with its link, as the standard carrier does (transport design 6.2): :link_down is
        // the last message its owner receives and the carrier's reference is dead afterwards. A :close, and a
        // peer that closed (TLS close_notify, or the connection dropped), are a normal end; a failed open, a
        // failed read (a fatal TLS alert, a reset) and a failed write end it with the link exit reasons of the
        // links design 6.3, so a supervisor restarts a faulted link and leaves a closed one alone. A failed
        // open is a refused connection or a failed handshake (the wrapper's tag 1; (:link_down, :fault)):
        // (:explicit, :link_unavailable); a dial address without a stated timeout is (:explicit, :bad_address);
        // a dial that timed out, in the connect or in the handshake, is (:explicit, :link_timeout) after
        // (:link_down, :timeout). stop_self takes effect when this dispatch returns.
        _: atom <- case link_down_now of {
            true -> case ended_normally of {
                true -> stop_self(:normal);
                false -> case open_failed of {
                    true -> case bad_address of {
                        true -> stop_self((:explicit, :bad_address));
                        false -> case connect_timed_out of {
                            true -> stop_self((:explicit, :link_timeout));
                            false -> stop_self((:explicit, :link_unavailable))
                        }
                    };
                    false -> stop_self((:explicit, :link_lost))
                }
            };
            false -> :ok
        };
        up_next: boolean <- case link_down_now of {
            true -> false;
            false -> state.up or connected or do_adopt
        };
        listening_next: boolean <- case link_down_now of {
            true -> false;
            false -> bound or state.listening
        };
        fd_next: int64 <- case kind == :open and adoptable == false of {
            true -> open_fd;
            false -> case link_down_now of { true -> 0 - 1; false -> state.fd }
        };
        credit_next: int64 <- case kind == :credit of {
            true -> state.rx_credit + msg_count(msg);
            false -> case got of { true -> state.rx_credit - 1; false -> state.rx_credit }
        };
        keep_polling: boolean <- connected or bound or do_adopt or (kind == :poll and (up_next or listening_next));
        poll_delay: uint64 <- case kind == :poll and up_next and listening_next == false and state.rx_credit == 0 of { true -> 20; false -> 1 };
        _: boolean <- case keep_polling of {
            true -> {
                sequence proc[concurrency]
                    _: timer_ref <- send_after(poll_delay, self(), :poll impl ActorMessage {})
                produces
                    pure true
                end
            };
            false -> true
        }
    produces
        pure (:no_reply, { owner: owner, fd: fd_next, rx_credit: credit_next, up: up_next, listening: listening_next })
    end
}
